.\" XSTRING(3) Manual Page
.TH XSTRING 3 "2026" "Extern Library" "Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
xstrlen, vxstrlen, xstrcmp, xstrcmb, xbasename \- compute formatted string length, compare strings, concatenate strings, and extract path basename
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <xstring.h>

size_t vxstrlen(const char *restrict fmt, va_list ap);
size_t xstrlen(const char *restrict fmt, ...);
int xstrcmp(const char * __s1, const char ** __s2, size_t n);
char *xstrcmb(const char *s1, const char *s2);
const char *xbasename(const char *path);
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION
The xstring library supplies helper routines that compute the number of
characters required to format a string, without writing any output and
without allocating memory.

These functions parallel the behavior of snprintf(3) and vsnprintf(3)
when called with a NULL buffer and a size of zero.

The library also provides a safe string concatenation helper that
allocates a new buffer for the combined result.

.SH vxstrlen()
vxstrlen() returns the number of characters that would be produced by
formatting the string described by \fIfmt\fP with the argument list
\fIap\fP.

On Windows systems, the function wraps \fB_vscprintf()\fP, which returns the
required buffer length.

On POSIX systems, the function uses:

.RS
vsnprintf(NULL, 0, fmt, ap)
.RE

The return value excludes the final NUL terminator.

If the formatted output would fail or cannot be determined, the function
returns 0.

.SH xstrlen()
xstrlen() is a convenience function wrapping vxstrlen(), accepting a
variadic argument list.

.SH xstrcmp()
xstrcmp() compares a single string (\fI__s1\fP) against an array of strings (\fI__s2\fP)
containing \fIn\fP elements. Returns 1 if a match is found, 0 otherwise.

Both \fI__s1\fP and array entries may be \fINULL\fP:

.IP "- Matching rules:"
.TP
* If both \fI__s1\fP and an array entry are NULL, it counts as a match.
* If only one is NULL, it is skipped.
* Otherwise, normal strcmp() comparison is used.

.SH xstrcmb()
xstrcmb() concatenates two C-style strings into a newly allocated string.

The returned buffer is allocated using malloc(3) and must be freed by the
caller using free(3).

Both input strings may be \fINULL\fP.

.IP "- Behavior:"
.TP
* If both \fIs1\fP and \fIs2\fP are NULL, the function returns NULL.
* If one argument is NULL, a duplicate of the other string is returned.
* If both arguments are non-NULL, the result contains \fIs1\fP followed by \fIs2\fP.
* On allocation failure, NULL is returned.

The function never modifies its input strings.

.SH xbasename()
xbasename() returns a pointer to the final component of the path
\fIpath\fP.

Both POSIX-style '/' and Windows-style '\\e' path separators are
recognized.

For example:

.nf
xbasename("/home/mrr/project/main.c");
.fi

returns a pointer to:

.nf
"main.c"
.fi

Windows-style paths are also supported:

.nf
xbasename("C:\\eUsers\\eMrR\\eproject\\emain.c");
.fi

returns:

.nf
"main.c"
.fi

Mixed path separators are supported:

.nf
xbasename("C:\\eUsers/MrR\\eproject/src/main.c");
.fi

returns:

.nf
"main.c"
.fi

If \fIpath\fP contains no path separator, \fIpath\fP itself is returned.

xbasename() does not allocate memory. The returned pointer refers directly
to the original \fIpath\fP string and must not be passed to free(3).

If \fIpath\fP is NULL, xbasename() returns NULL.

A path ending in a separator produces an empty basename.

.SH RETURN VALUES
vxstrlen() and xstrlen() return the number of characters, excluding the
terminating NUL, or 0 if a formatting error occurred or \fIfmt\fP is NULL.

xstrcmp() returns 1 if \fI__s1\fP matches any element of \fI__s2\fP, 0 otherwise.

xstrcmb() returns a pointer to a newly allocated string, or NULL on error
or if both input strings are NULL.

xbasename() returns a pointer to the final path component within
\fIpath\fP, or \fIpath\fP itself when no separator is present.
If \fIpath\fP is NULL, NULL is returned.

None of these functions set errno.

.SH ERRORS
Formatting errors result in a return value of 0. No additional error
information is provided.

xstrcmb() reports allocation failure by returning NULL.

xbasename() does not allocate memory and does not report errors through
errno.

.SH PORTABILITY
Windows uses the native \fB_vscprintf()\fP routine.
POSIX uses \fBvsnprintf(NULL,0,...)\fP.

The interface is portable across Windows, Linux, BSD, and macOS.

xbasename() recognizes both '/' and '\\e' path separators regardless of
the host operating system.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
size_t len = xstrlen("%s %d", "value", 10);
char *buf = malloc(len + 1);
sprintf(buf, "%s %d", "value", 10);

const char *arr[] = {"fff", "333", NULL};
int result;

result = xstrcmp("fff", arr, 3);   // returns 1
result = xstrcmp("abc", arr, 3);   // returns 0
result = xstrcmp(NULL, arr, 3);    // returns 0
result = xstrcmp(NULL, (const char*[]){NULL, "foo"}, 2); // returns 1

char *s = xstrcmb("hello", "world");
if (s) {
    printf("%s\n", s);
    free(s);
}

const char *name =
    xbasename("C:\\eproject\\esrc\\exstring.c");

printf("%s\n", name); // xstring.c
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO
snprintf(3), vsnprintf(3), strlen(3), strcmp(3), strrchr(3), malloc(3), free(3)

